window.__LIVE_NEWS__["2026-11-28"] = {"date":"2026-11-28","articles":[{"id":"n1795854676_0","date":"2026-11-28","source":"BBC","country":"UK","title":"Spanish PM and rivals rally supporters in final pitch for votes","summary":"Prime Minister Pedro Sánchez wants to defy the polls, and his main rivals accuse him of not telling the truth and of \"betrayal\".","body":"BBC News Spanish PM and rivals rally supporters in final pitch for votes Image source, Reuters Image caption, Pedro Sánchez accused his opponents of hurling insults and lies but his conservative rival has promised a new era of politics\n\nSpain&#x27;s main party leaders have held their final rallies ahead of Sunday&#x27;s election, with Socialist Prime Minister Pedro Sánchez saying he is confident of defying the polls.\n\nI cannot imagine us losing, he said, posing for selfies with supporters in red T-shirts and accusing conservative rival Alberto Núñez Feijóo of planning a coalition with the far right.\n\nFeijóo&#x27;s People&#x27;s Party (PP) is narrowly ahead in the polls, and in the final hours of the campaign he travelled first to Barcelona and then Seville, promising voters a new political era of truth, decency and good governance .\n\nHe accused Sánchez of evading questions on key issues like corruption, rail disasters, immigration and the national debt.\n\nImage source, Reuters Image caption, Alberto Núñez Feijóo&#x27;s PP holds a narrow lead in the opinion polls\n\nThe Socialist leader heads one of Europe&#x27;s few left-wing governments, and with the French presidential elections five months away, there is a real chance that two of the continent&#x27;s biggest economies could move to the right in quick succession.\n\nAlmost 39 million people have a vote in Spain and while commentators expect a high turnout, most polls have suggested a close finish. Under Spanish electoral law, no polls have been published since Monday.\n\nThis time, yes, we&#x27;re going to do it, Feijóo told PP supporters, in a nod to his failure to form a government three years ago despite winning most seats.\n\nImage source, Reuters Image caption, Abascal said on Friday that Sánchez&#x27;s government had &#x27;betrayed&#x27; Spaniards\n\nIf he gets to form a government this time, it will be with the support of anti-immigration party Vox, led by Santiago Abascal, whose final rally took place in front of thousands of people in a bull-ring in Valencia on Spain&#x27;s east coast.\n\nThe venue was filled with Spanish flags and green Vox banners, and one of the biggest cheers of the night came when the secretary general of the party called for less burka and less halal .\n\nIgnacio Garriga said he was not acting out of hatred but love for the homeland.\n\nA small section of the crowd could be heard chanting Viva Franco in reference to the dictator, whose death in 1975 heralded the start of Spain&#x27;s transition to democracy.\n\nA number of first-time voters at the rally said they were supporting Vox because of its policies on immigration.\n\nLledó, 18, said what had happened in Ceuta in the summer, when more than 70,000 migrants crossed into the Spanish territory from Morocco, showed Sánchez was not able to control the country&#x27;s border.\n\nShe had come to the bullring with her mum and aunt, Carli Chilet, who argued that Vox are the best for women&#x27;s rights .\n\nVox is hoping to almost double its seats from the 33 it won three years ago. It backs policies such as national priority - putting Spanish citizens first for welfare - and remigration for foreigners not considered to be contributing to the economy.\n\nImage caption, Lledó attended the rally with her family\n\nLeaders clash in final Spain election debate as housing dominates run-in\n\nPublished 1 day ago Spanish election: What you need to know and what&#x27;s at stake\n\nPublished 19 hours ago Who to watch in Spain&#x27;s election race\n\nPublished 19 hours ago Clashing political visions reflect a divided Spain ahead of election\n\nPublished 3 days ago The final day of campaigning was very much in the shadow of a four-way TV debate the night before that exposed Spain&#x27;s sharply polarised politics.\n\nAll four leaders had solutions for the housing crisis. Feijóo said the PP would build a million homes over four years, while Sánchez said housing should be a right not an object of speculation and profit.\n\nTo play this video you need to enable JavaScript in your browser. This video can not be played\n\nWatch: Could housing decide Spain&#x27;s general election?\n\nWhile the conservatives tried to turn the spotlight on the series of scandals that have overshadowed the Socialists&#x27; term in office, Sánchez and his left-wing ally Ada Colau, who heads Frente Amplio (The Broad Front), focused on women&#x27;s rights.\n\nThe two leaders seized on a moment in the debate when the PP leader accused Colau and the rest of the left of staying quiet during a flood disaster in Valencia, briefly moving his finger to his lips. The word he used calladita is a diminutive and can also be used to tell a woman to keep quiet in a condescending manner.\n\nSánchez reacted immediately and called on him to show a little respect , accusing him of sexism.\n\nFeijóo sought to clarify what he meant on Friday, and accused Ada Colau of remaining silent during these years of corruption . His decision to wind up his campaign in Seville was significant, because the wider southern region of Andalusia has 61 seats in Spain&#x27;s 350-seat parliament, more than any other.\n\nAndalusia is also important because it is run by a coalition made up of the PP and Abascal&#x27;s Vox, as are three other regions, Extremadura, Aragón, and Castilla y León.\n\nFeijóo has insisted he wants to govern with a majority, backed up by parliamentary support agreements with Vox and says he has never spoken to Abascal about giving Vox ministerial power.\n\nGoverning is about managing, and I have spent my whole life preparing for this moment, he said in Seville.\n\nOne of his new supporters at the rally was a retired woman called Isabel who told Spanish public broadvaster RTVE that she was attending a PP rally for the first time. The formerly loyal Socialist voter said she was disillusioned with a party led by Sánchez. Another voter at the rally said Spain needed a good manager .\n\nImage source, EPA Image caption, Colau is a former mayor of Barcelona\n\nSánchez took to the stage in Madrid with his wife Begoña Gómez, and donned a red T-shirt that read power of the people .\n\nHe then went on to praise Colau and her left-wing coalition&#x27;s campaign.\n\nThese elections are about moving Spain forward, not taking it backwards, he said.\n\nSocialist supporter Leon Felipe, 25, told Reuters news agency: Well, I think what&#x27;s at stake in these elections is a big deal, is not a change of government... but rather a return to the past - a return to Franco&#x27;s dictatorship.\n\nAs he was speaking, Colau told a large audience in the south of Madrid that not only would they not remain silent, we will win , vowing to form another progressive government .\n\nWatch our pick of standout clips from across the BBC\n\nPrevious Next 0:21 The Premier League footballer allergic to grass... 00:00:21, play video The Premier League footballer allergic to grass... 1:16 My 15-year-old son dropped his cup of tea and just died. 00:01:16, play video My 15-year-old son dropped his cup of tea and just died 0:47 Father and son set three driving world records. 00:00:47, play video Father and son set three driving world records 0:55 Co-op Live gig targeted by organised criminals, say police. 00:00:55, play video Co-op Live gig targeted by organised criminals, say police 0:48 What&#x27;s behind the surge in flu? 00:00:48, play video What&#x27;s behind the surge in flu? 1:47 Zelensky questions &#x27;strange&#x27; Trump-Putin friendship. 00:01:47, play video Zelensky questions &#x27;strange&#x27; Trump-Putin friendship 0:58 Suspected rogue trader charged man £9,153 for outdoor socket. 00:00:58, play video Suspected rogue trader charged man £9,153 for outdoor socket 0:35 Four apes die at Twycross Zoo. 00:00:35, play video Four apes die at Twycross Zoo 0:55 Are Black Friday deals really as good as they seem? 00:00:55, play video Are Black Friday deals really as good as they seem? 0:55 Catherine joins cancer support group in new documentary. 00:00:55, play video Catherine joins cancer support group in new documentary 1:20 Student meets man who saved her from Huntingdon train attacker. 00:01:20, play video Student meets man who saved her from Huntingdon train attacker 0:21 Moment semi-truck loses control in US wet weather. 00:00:21, play video Moment semi-truck loses control in US wet weather 0:22 Jose Mourinho jokes with journalist cousin at presser. 00:00:22, play video Jose Mourinho jokes with journalist cousin at presser 2:13 Man wrongly sentenced to death says Met Police ruined his life. 00:02:13, play video Man wrongly sentenced to death says Met Police ruined his life 1:05 How much is rent in Birmingham? 00:01:05, play video How much is rent in Birmingham? 0:51 Inside the control room forecasting your weather. 00:00:51, play video Inside the control room forecasting your weather 1:24 &#x27;It&#x27;s impossible&#x27;: Spain&#x27;s young people on housing. 00:01:24, play video &#x27;It&#x27;s impossible&#x27;: Spain&#x27;s young people on housing 1:32 &#x27;We&#x27;re scared to turn our heating on&#x27; 00:01:32, play video &#x27;We&#x27;re scared to turn our heating on&#x27; 1:02 Why we&#x27;re swapping screens for scenery. 00:01:02, play video Why we&#x27;re swapping screens for scenery 1:09 &#x27;I&#x27;d be dead if it wasn&#x27;t for my binmen&#x27; 00:01:09, play video &#x27;I&#x27;d be dead if it wasn&#x27;t for my binmen&#x27; Top stories Pensions and benefits services still down after &#x27;cyber incident&#x27;\n\nCatherine&#x27;s emotional cancer documentary strips away the stiff upper lip\n\nTwo staff killed in Israeli strike on Gaza medical clinic, MSF says\n\nThe Papers: &#x27;Catherine on cancer fight&#x27; and &#x27;Benefits and pensions chaos&#x27;\n\nA clinic is reassessing people with ADHD and autism diagnoses - but critics fear it could deepen stigma\n\n&#x27;It feels very therapeutic&#x27;: The young hikers swapping screens for walking groups\n\nWeekly quiz: Which music icon&#x27;s house did Miley Cyrus get married at?\n\nMy self-care ritual is a meal at a fancy restaurant - on my own\n\nPregnant US inmate escapes in prison vehicle after medical appointment\n\nThe US pastor who wants to see women&#x27;s right to vote overturned\n\nDesperate appeals from women on death row in Iran after protests\n\nFirst look: Strike and Robin return to take on a dangerous cult\n\nDrift off to sleep with a little help from Bob Mortimer\n\nThe extraordinary journey of the humble banana\n\nHow can vitamin D help us in the winter?\n\nCharity in Capt Tom&#x27;s name bids to be struck off\n\n&#x27;Catherine on cancer fight&#x27; and &#x27;Benefits and pensions chaos&#x27;\n\nAustralian wrestler Bronson Reed dies aged 38, WWE says\n\nPharmacies get new powers to swap prescriptions during shortages\n\nCatherine&#x27;s emotional cancer documentary strips away the stiff upper lip\n\nPensions and benefits services still down after &#x27;cyber incident&#x27;\n\nCarly Rae Jepsen&#x27;s viral hit proves what fans knew all along\n\nSet time aside for children to read for pleasure, schools to be told\n\nA clinic is reassessing people with ADHD and autism diagnoses - but critics fear it could deepen stigma\n\nCopyright © 2026 BBC. The BBC is not responsible for the content of external sites. Read about our approach to external linking.","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c8wy5p4gdnwvo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss","source_type":"live","vocabPoints":[{"word":"extraordinary","phonetic":"","definition":"a. 非常的, 特别的, 非凡的\\ / [经] 非常的, 特别的, 临时的","example":"My self-care ritual is a meal at a fancy restaurant - on my own\n\nPregnant US inmate escapes in prison vehicle after medical appointment\n\nThe US pastor who wants to see women&#x27;s right to vote overturned\n\nDesperate app","example2":"The extraordinary situation has drawn widespread attention.","en_def":"a. beyond what is ordinary or usual; highly unusual or exceptional or remarkable；s. far more than usual or expected"},{"word":"conservative","phonetic":"","definition":"a. 保守的, 守旧的, 有保存力的\\ / [化] 防腐剂; 保存剂","example":"BBC News Spanish PM and rivals rally supporters in final pitch for votes Image source, Reuters Image caption, Pedro Sánchez accused his opponents of hurling insults and lies but his conservative rival has promised a new ","example2":"The report highlighted conservative as a key factor in the decision.","en_def":"a person who is reluctant to accept changes and new ideas；a member of a Conservative Party"},{"word":"dictatorship","phonetic":"","definition":"n. 独裁者之职位, 独裁, 独裁政权\\ / [法] 专攻, 独裁权","example":"but rather a return to the past - a return to Franco&#x27;s dictatorship.","example2":"The latest figures show a clear link between the economy and dictatorship.","en_def":"a form of government in which the ruler is an absolute dictator (not restricted by a constitution or laws or opposition etc.)"}],"grammarPoints":[{"pattern":"被动语态 (Passive Voice)","explanation":"be 动词 + 过去分词。新闻大量使用被动语态，因为动作的承受者比执行者更重要。注意 by 短语引出真正的执行者，常被省略。","en":"The passive voice emphasizes the action or its receiver rather than the doer: be + past participle. Used heavily in news when the doer is unknown or less important. The agent can be added with 'by' or omitted entirely.","example":"Under Spanish electoral law, no polls have been published since Monday.","example2":"The bridge was built in 1932 and is still used by thousands of commuters every day."}],"slangPoints":[{"term":"in an effort to","meaning":"为了；以期","en":"Usual usage in everyday/news English — \"Sanctions were eased in an effort to restart talks.\"","example":"","example2":"Sanctions were eased in an effort to restart talks."}]},{"id":"n1795854676_1","date":"2026-11-28","source":"BBC","country":"UK","title":"Turkish officials 'destroyed' pro-opposition TV channel, owner tells BBC","summary":"The headquarters of Halk TV were raided last month, the latest in a series of crackdowns on news sources deemed critical of the government.","body":"BBC News Turkish officials &#x27;destroyed&#x27; pro-opposition TV channel, owner tells BBC Image source, Getty Images Image caption, Cafer Mahiroğlu says Halk TV has been destroyed by Turkish authorities\n\nTurkish authorities have destroyed one of the country&#x27;s most-watched news channels, its London-based owner has told the BBC.\n\nSpeaking to the BBC&#x27;s Newshour, Cafer Mahiroğlu said that nine people connected to Halk TV were arrested this week on charges including money laundering, bribery and spreading misleading information.\n\nMahiroğlu said that several of his family members were among those arrested when the pro-opposition channel was taken into government control last month, adding that the allegations against him and Halk TV are completely wrong .\n\nThe Istanbul Chief Public Prosecutor&#x27; Office said that two former news anchors of the channel were among the most recent group of people detained.\n\nIn a statement, prosecutors accused the anchors of producing false reports in exchange for money.\n\nAuthorities have also issued an arrest warrant for the channel&#x27;s news coordinator Kürşad Oğuz who is abroad , the statement said, without specifying where.\n\nMahiroğlu told the BBC that he could be sent to prison if he tried to return to Turkey.\n\n[The government] wants to be in control of the whole media... I&#x27;m more worried about my country and my democracy than my channel, he said.\n\nThe headquarters of Halk TV was raided by Turkish authorities last month , halting the channel&#x27;s live broadcasts, the latest in a series of crackdowns on news sources deemed to be critical of Turkish President Tayyip Erdoğan and his administration.\n\nThe government subsequently appointed a new administrator to run the channel.\n\nErdoğan&#x27;s government controls much of the country&#x27;s media, which ranks among the worst globally for press freedom , external , though the government insists that it does not detain media workers for carrying out journalistic activities.\n\nHowever, the president has scaled up a crackdown on dissent since his narrow victory in the 2023 presidential election.\n\nEarlier this month, authorities took control of pro-opposition network Sozcu TV.\n\nErdoğan&#x27;s main challenger, mayor of Istanbul Ekrem Imamoğlu, has been jailed on corruption charges and for violating election laws , which he and his party have fiercely denied.\n\nHundreds of Imamoğlu&#x27;s supporters have faced similar accusations, while the government has also imprisoned other campaigners, lawyers and comedians.\n\nStand-up comic held for jokes about Erdoğan and Islam in Turkey crackdown\n\nFierce protests in Turkey after Erdogan rival jailed\n\nThe video playlist Watch our pick of standout clips from across the BBC\n\nPrevious Next 0:21 The Premier League footballer allergic to grass... 00:00:21, play video The Premier League footballer allergic to grass... 1:16 My 15-year-old son dropped his cup of tea and just died. 00:01:16, play video My 15-year-old son dropped his cup of tea and just died 0:47 Father and son set three driving world records. 00:00:47, play video Father and son set three driving world records 0:55 Co-op Live gig targeted by organised criminals, say police. 00:00:55, play video Co-op Live gig targeted by organised criminals, say police 0:48 What&#x27;s behind the surge in flu? 00:00:48, play video What&#x27;s behind the surge in flu? 1:47 Zelensky questions &#x27;strange&#x27; Trump-Putin friendship. 00:01:47, play video Zelensky questions &#x27;strange&#x27; Trump-Putin friendship 0:58 Suspected rogue trader charged man £9,153 for outdoor socket. 00:00:58, play video Suspected rogue trader charged man £9,153 for outdoor socket 0:35 Four apes die at Twycross Zoo. 00:00:35, play video Four apes die at Twycross Zoo 0:55 Are Black Friday deals really as good as they seem? 00:00:55, play video Are Black Friday deals really as good as they seem? 0:55 Catherine joins cancer support group in new documentary. 00:00:55, play video Catherine joins cancer support group in new documentary 1:20 Student meets man who saved her from Huntingdon train attacker. 00:01:20, play video Student meets man who saved her from Huntingdon train attacker 0:21 Moment semi-truck loses control in US wet weather. 00:00:21, play video Moment semi-truck loses control in US wet weather 0:22 Jose Mourinho jokes with journalist cousin at presser. 00:00:22, play video Jose Mourinho jokes with journalist cousin at presser 2:13 Man wrongly sentenced to death says Met Police ruined his life. 00:02:13, play video Man wrongly sentenced to death says Met Police ruined his life 1:05 How much is rent in Birmingham? 00:01:05, play video How much is rent in Birmingham? 0:51 Inside the control room forecasting your weather. 00:00:51, play video Inside the control room forecasting your weather 1:24 &#x27;It&#x27;s impossible&#x27;: Spain&#x27;s young people on housing. 00:01:24, play video &#x27;It&#x27;s impossible&#x27;: Spain&#x27;s young people on housing 1:32 &#x27;We&#x27;re scared to turn our heating on&#x27; 00:01:32, play video &#x27;We&#x27;re scared to turn our heating on&#x27; 1:02 Why we&#x27;re swapping screens for scenery. 00:01:02, play video Why we&#x27;re swapping screens for scenery 1:09 &#x27;I&#x27;d be dead if it wasn&#x27;t for my binmen&#x27; 00:01:09, play video &#x27;I&#x27;d be dead if it wasn&#x27;t for my binmen&#x27; Top stories Pensions and benefits services still down after &#x27;cyber incident&#x27;\n\nCatherine&#x27;s emotional cancer documentary strips away the stiff upper lip\n\nTwo staff killed in Israeli strike on Gaza medical clinic, MSF says\n\nThe Papers: &#x27;Catherine on cancer fight&#x27; and &#x27;Benefits and pensions chaos&#x27;\n\nA clinic is reassessing people with ADHD and autism diagnoses - but critics fear it could deepen stigma\n\n&#x27;It feels very therapeutic&#x27;: The young hikers swapping screens for walking groups\n\nWeekly quiz: Which music icon&#x27;s house did Miley Cyrus get married at?\n\nMy self-care ritual is a meal at a fancy restaurant - on my own\n\nPregnant US inmate escapes in prison vehicle after medical appointment\n\nThe US pastor who wants to see women&#x27;s right to vote overturned\n\nDesperate appeals from women on death row in Iran after protests\n\nFirst look: Strike and Robin return to take on a dangerous cult\n\nDrift off to sleep with a little help from Bob Mortimer\n\nThe extraordinary journey of the humble banana\n\nHow can vitamin D help us in the winter?\n\nCharity in Capt Tom&#x27;s name bids to be struck off\n\n&#x27;Catherine on cancer fight&#x27; and &#x27;Benefits and pensions chaos&#x27;\n\nAustralian wrestler Bronson Reed dies aged 38, WWE says\n\nPharmacies get new powers to swap prescriptions during shortages\n\nCatherine&#x27;s emotional cancer documentary strips away the stiff upper lip\n\nPensions and benefits services still down after &#x27;cyber incident&#x27;\n\nCarly Rae Jepsen&#x27;s viral hit proves what fans knew all along\n\nSet time aside for children to read for pleasure, schools to be told\n\nA clinic is reassessing people with ADHD and autism diagnoses - but critics fear it could deepen stigma\n\nCopyright © 2026 BBC. The BBC is not responsible for the content of external sites. Read about our approach to external linking.","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cq36gk151xkdo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss","source_type":"live","vocabPoints":[{"word":"administration","phonetic":"","definition":"政府；管理","example":"The headquarters of Halk TV was raided by Turkish authorities last month , halting the channel&#x27;s live broadcasts, the latest in a series of crackdowns on news sources deemed to be critical of Turkish President Tayyi","example2":"The latest figures show a clear link between the economy and administration.","en_def":"a method of tending to or managing the affairs of a some group of people (especially the group's business affairs)；the persons (or committees or departments…"},{"word":"administrator","phonetic":"","definition":"n. 管理人, 行政官\\ / [经] 遗产管理人员","example":"The government subsequently appointed a new administrator to run the channel.","example2":"The debate over administrator is unlikely to end soon.","en_def":"someone who administers a business；the party appointed by a probate court to distribute the estate of someone who dies without a will or without naming an ex…"},{"word":"authorities","phonetic":"/ɔːˈθɒrətiz/","definition":"当局","example":"BBC News Turkish officials &#x27;destroyed&#x27; pro-opposition TV channel, owner tells BBC Image source, Getty Images Image caption, Cafer Mahiroğlu says Halk TV has been destroyed by Turkish authorities\n\nTurkish author","_bank_example":"Authorities urged calm.","example2":"The authorities have launched a full investigation into the incident.","en_def":"the people or organizations that have official power and responsibility"}],"grammarPoints":[{"pattern":"间接引语 / 转述动词 (Reported Speech)","explanation":"said/told/warned/confirmed + that 从句（that 常省略）。新闻标题后正文常用转述动词引述当事人观点，注意从句语序不倒装。","en":"Reported (indirect) speech reports what someone said without using their exact words, typically with a reporting verb (said, told, added) and a tense shift: 'She said she was leaving.'","example":"BBC News Turkish officials &#x27;destroyed&#x27; pro-opposition TV channel, owner tells BBC Image source, Getty Images Image caption, Cafer Mahiroğlu says Halk TV has been destroyed by Turkish authorities\n\nTurkish author","example2":"She told reporters (that) the deal had already been signed."}],"slangPoints":[{"term":"on the brink of","meaning":"濒临……的边缘","en":"very close to something (often a crisis or change) e.g. \"The country was on the brink of civil war.\"","example":"","example2":"The country was on the brink of civil war."}]},{"id":"n1795854676_2","date":"2026-11-28","source":"BBC","country":"UK","title":"Eight killed in Peruvian military helicopter crash","summary":"The pilot was the sole survivor of the crash during a rescue mission in a remote area known for drug trafficking.","body":"BBC News Eight killed in Peruvian military helicopter crash Image source, AFP via Getty Images Image caption, A Peruvian military helicopter crashed during a rescue mission\n\nEight military personnel have been killed in a helicopter crash in a remote area of Peru known for growing and trafficking drugs.\n\nAuthorities say the aircraft came down near the town of Sanabamba during a mission to evacuate an army patrol in the valley of the Apurimac, Ene, and Mantaro rivers, known by its Spanish acronym VRAEM.\n\nOften referred to as &#x27;Cocaine Valley&#x27;, the area is Peru&#x27;s largest coca-growing valley where gangs and the remnants of the former Shining Path rebel group operate.\n\nThe Peruvian ministry of defence said the pilot was the sole survivor of the crash, the cause of which is still unknown.\n\nIn its statement, the ministry said search and rescue operations located the aircraft and the nine military personnel who were on board .\n\nIt named the pilot as Enzo Espinoza Roque and said he was evacuated to Lima to receive specialised medical treatment .\n\nThe aircraft was reported missing on Thursday night after rescuing three army personnel.\n\nNo further information was shared about why the group needed to be rescued, but it was confirmed that they were among those who were killed in the crash.\n\nJoint Command of the Peruvian Armed Forces posted a tribute online extending deepest condolences to the family members, friends, and comrades-in-arms of the members of our VRAEM Special Command who lost their lives while carrying out a military mission. May God keep them in His glory .\n\nIn February, a MI-17 helicopter crashed in southern Peru in February killing all 15 on board.\n\nPeru, along with Colombia, is one of the world&#x27;s largest producers of coca leaves and cocaine , according to the United Nations.\n\nFifteen killed after helicopter crashes during Peru flood rescue\n\nPope Leo rides in helicopter cockpit during Peru trip\n\nThe video playlist Watch our pick of standout clips from across the BBC\n\nPrevious Next 0:21 The Premier League footballer allergic to grass... 00:00:21, play video The Premier League footballer allergic to grass... 1:16 My 15-year-old son dropped his cup of tea and just died. 00:01:16, play video My 15-year-old son dropped his cup of tea and just died 0:47 Father and son set three driving world records. 00:00:47, play video Father and son set three driving world records 0:55 Co-op Live gig targeted by organised criminals, say police. 00:00:55, play video Co-op Live gig targeted by organised criminals, say police 0:48 What&#x27;s behind the surge in flu? 00:00:48, play video What&#x27;s behind the surge in flu? 1:47 Zelensky questions &#x27;strange&#x27; Trump-Putin friendship. 00:01:47, play video Zelensky questions &#x27;strange&#x27; Trump-Putin friendship 0:58 Suspected rogue trader charged man £9,153 for outdoor socket. 00:00:58, play video Suspected rogue trader charged man £9,153 for outdoor socket 0:35 Four apes die at Twycross Zoo. 00:00:35, play video Four apes die at Twycross Zoo 0:55 Are Black Friday deals really as good as they seem? 00:00:55, play video Are Black Friday deals really as good as they seem? 0:55 Catherine joins cancer support group in new documentary. 00:00:55, play video Catherine joins cancer support group in new documentary 1:20 Student meets man who saved her from Huntingdon train attacker. 00:01:20, play video Student meets man who saved her from Huntingdon train attacker 0:21 Moment semi-truck loses control in US wet weather. 00:00:21, play video Moment semi-truck loses control in US wet weather 0:22 Jose Mourinho jokes with journalist cousin at presser. 00:00:22, play video Jose Mourinho jokes with journalist cousin at presser 2:13 Man wrongly sentenced to death says Met Police ruined his life. 00:02:13, play video Man wrongly sentenced to death says Met Police ruined his life 1:05 How much is rent in Birmingham? 00:01:05, play video How much is rent in Birmingham? 0:51 Inside the control room forecasting your weather. 00:00:51, play video Inside the control room forecasting your weather 1:24 &#x27;It&#x27;s impossible&#x27;: Spain&#x27;s young people on housing. 00:01:24, play video &#x27;It&#x27;s impossible&#x27;: Spain&#x27;s young people on housing 1:32 &#x27;We&#x27;re scared to turn our heating on&#x27; 00:01:32, play video &#x27;We&#x27;re scared to turn our heating on&#x27; 1:02 Why we&#x27;re swapping screens for scenery. 00:01:02, play video Why we&#x27;re swapping screens for scenery 1:09 &#x27;I&#x27;d be dead if it wasn&#x27;t for my binmen&#x27; 00:01:09, play video &#x27;I&#x27;d be dead if it wasn&#x27;t for my binmen&#x27; Top stories Pensions and benefits services still down after &#x27;cyber incident&#x27;\n\nCatherine&#x27;s emotional cancer documentary strips away the stiff upper lip\n\nTwo staff killed in Israeli strike on Gaza medical clinic, MSF says\n\nThe Papers: &#x27;Catherine on cancer fight&#x27; and &#x27;Benefits and pensions chaos&#x27;\n\nA clinic is reassessing people with ADHD and autism diagnoses - but critics fear it could deepen stigma\n\n&#x27;It feels very therapeutic&#x27;: The young hikers swapping screens for walking groups\n\nWeekly quiz: Which music icon&#x27;s house did Miley Cyrus get married at?\n\nMy self-care ritual is a meal at a fancy restaurant - on my own\n\nPregnant US inmate escapes in prison vehicle after medical appointment\n\nThe US pastor who wants to see women&#x27;s right to vote overturned\n\nDesperate appeals from women on death row in Iran after protests\n\nFirst look: Strike and Robin return to take on a dangerous cult\n\nDrift off to sleep with a little help from Bob Mortimer\n\nThe extraordinary journey of the humble banana\n\nHow can vitamin D help us in the winter?\n\nCharity in Capt Tom&#x27;s name bids to be struck off\n\n&#x27;Catherine on cancer fight&#x27; and &#x27;Benefits and pensions chaos&#x27;\n\nAustralian wrestler Bronson Reed dies aged 38, WWE says\n\nPharmacies get new powers to swap prescriptions during shortages\n\nCatherine&#x27;s emotional cancer documentary strips away the stiff upper lip\n\nPensions and benefits services still down after &#x27;cyber incident&#x27;\n\nCarly Rae Jepsen&#x27;s viral hit proves what fans knew all along\n\nSet time aside for children to read for pleasure, schools to be told\n\nA clinic is reassessing people with ADHD and autism diagnoses - but critics fear it could deepen stigma\n\nCopyright © 2026 BBC. The BBC is not responsible for the content of external sites. Read about our approach to external linking.","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cwp9d85mlp88o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss","source_type":"live","vocabPoints":[{"word":"trafficking","phonetic":"/ˈtræfɪkɪŋ/","definition":"非法贩运（人口/毒品）","example":"BBC News Eight killed in Peruvian military helicopter crash Image source, AFP via Getty Images Image caption, A Peruvian military helicopter crashed during a rescue mission\n\nEight military personnel have been killed in a","_bank_example":"Police broke up a trafficking ring.","example2":"Police broke up a trafficking ring."},{"word":"condolences","phonetic":"/kənˈdəʊlənsɪz/","definition":"哀悼；慰问","example":"Joint Command of the Peruvian Armed Forces posted a tribute online extending deepest condolences to the family members, friends, and comrades-in-arms of the members of our VRAEM Special Command who lost their lives while","_bank_example":"The president sent his condolences.","example2":"The president sent his condolences."},{"word":"documentary","phonetic":"","definition":"n. 记录片\\ / a. 文件的","example":"0:55 Catherine joins cancer support group in new documentary.","example2":"Many voters expressed documentary support for the new measure.","en_def":"a film or TV program presenting the facts about a person or event；a. relating to or consisting of or derived from documents"}],"grammarPoints":[{"pattern":"定语从句 (Relative Clause)","explanation":"who/which/that 引导的定语从句修饰前面的名词。who 指人、which 指物，that 两者皆可；关系代词在从句中作宾语时可省略。","en":"A relative clause modifies a noun using who (people), which (things), or that (both). When the relative pronoun is the object it can be omitted: 'the law (that) the Senate passed'.","example":"The Peruvian ministry of defence said the pilot was the sole survivor of the crash, the cause of which is still unknown.","example2":"The scientist who developed the vaccine won the Nobel Prize."}],"slangPoints":[{"term":"hold talks","meaning":"举行会谈（hold talks with…）","en":"\"hold talks\" — a common English phrase; typical collocation: \"hold talks with\".","example":"","example2":"The two leaders held talks behind closed doors."}]},{"id":"n1795854676_3","date":"2026-11-28","source":"BBC","country":"UK","title":"'Poison seller' Kenneth Law apologises in court to the families of his victims","summary":"The Canadian man, who pleaded guilty to 14 counts of aiding and abetting suicide earlier this year, said he was remorseful for his actions.","body":"BBC News &#x27;Poison seller&#x27; Kenneth Law apologises in court to the families of his victims Image source, PA Media By Brandon Drenon Published 27 November 2026 Updated 7 hours ago The Canadian man who sold toxic chemicals through online suicide forums in 40 countries apologised in court to the families of the victims, saying he deeply regrets his actions and will never repeat them.\n\nKenneth Law made the remarks on Friday during a sentencing hearing where prosecutors asked a judge to give him a 25-year prison term.\n\nThe 61-year-old pleaded guilty in May to 14 counts of aiding the suicide deaths of 14 people in Ontario, Canada, though he has admitted to causing the deaths of dozens of victims in England and Wales.\n\nHe will not face charges in the UK or over any British deaths under a plea deal which saw Canadian authorities withdraw more serious charges of murder.\n\nDuring Friday&#x27;s sentencing hearing in an Ontario court, prosecutors stressed that many of his victims were young and struggling with mental distress.\n\nIf Law receives the prosecution&#x27;s recommended sentence, he would serve just under 20 years in prison, after accounting for credit for time served since his May 2023 arrest.\n\nAs she sought to make her case in court on Friday, Crown Attorney Kathleen Doherty described Law as a predator taking advantage of vulnerable people .\n\nWhat they needed was help. But what they found was Mr Law and his products for death, Doherty said.\n\nShe also said Law&#x27;s motives were financial.\n\nIf people could pay, he sold it to them. There&#x27;s no discussion or inquiries or anything going on whereby Mr Law would have any ability to appreciate the circumstances of the individual he was dealing with, she said.\n\nLaw&#x27;s defence lawyer, Matthew Gourlay, countered those claims, arguing that his client was not responsible for any of their battles with mental illness..\n\nEach of these incidents is its own unique tragedy, Gourlay said. Mr Law may be a common denominator between them, but Mr Law was not the cause of the struggles and hardships that caused all these victims to seek out his products in the first place.\n\nHe said Law held a misguided belief he was helping his victims\n\nLaw&#x27;s defence attorney has asked the court to give him a 12-year sentence.\n\nAt the end of the hearing, Law told the court that he had deep regret for aiding in the suicide deaths of his victims.\n\nTo the families of the victims, words can never replace the loss of your loved ones. I convey my deepest condolences. I remain remorseful. I&#x27;m sorry.\n\nThe sentencing hearing followed an emotional few days of testimony in September, when grieving family members and friends told the court of the pain Law had caused.\n\nThose impact statements will be taken into account at sentencing, which is due at a later date.\n\nAuthorities said the former chef sold about 1,200 packages of toxic substances to recipients - who he met in online suicide forums - in 40 countries, roughly a quarter of which were sent to the UK.\n\nIf you or anyone you know has been affected by the issues raised in this article, details of organisations offering information are available at BBC Action Line\n\n&#x27;Poison seller&#x27; who sold toxic chemicals online to people across world admits aiding suicides\n\nMan who sold toxic chemicals should rot in hell, says mother of woman who killed herself\n\nSurvivor of Canadian poison seller says he exploited her despair\n\nThe video playlist Watch our pick of standout clips from across the BBC\n\nPrevious Next 0:21 The Premier League footballer allergic to grass... 00:00:21, play video The Premier League footballer allergic to grass... 1:16 My 15-year-old son dropped his cup of tea and just died. 00:01:16, play video My 15-year-old son dropped his cup of tea and just died 0:47 Father and son set three driving world records. 00:00:47, play video Father and son set three driving world records 0:55 Co-op Live gig targeted by organised criminals, say police. 00:00:55, play video Co-op Live gig targeted by organised criminals, say police 0:48 What&#x27;s behind the surge in flu? 00:00:48, play video What&#x27;s behind the surge in flu? 1:47 Zelensky questions &#x27;strange&#x27; Trump-Putin friendship. 00:01:47, play video Zelensky questions &#x27;strange&#x27; Trump-Putin friendship 0:58 Suspected rogue trader charged man £9,153 for outdoor socket. 00:00:58, play video Suspected rogue trader charged man £9,153 for outdoor socket 0:35 Four apes die at Twycross Zoo. 00:00:35, play video Four apes die at Twycross Zoo 0:55 Are Black Friday deals really as good as they seem? 00:00:55, play video Are Black Friday deals really as good as they seem? 0:55 Catherine joins cancer support group in new documentary. 00:00:55, play video Catherine joins cancer support group in new documentary 1:20 Student meets man who saved her from Huntingdon train attacker. 00:01:20, play video Student meets man who saved her from Huntingdon train attacker 0:21 Moment semi-truck loses control in US wet weather. 00:00:21, play video Moment semi-truck loses control in US wet weather 0:22 Jose Mourinho jokes with journalist cousin at presser. 00:00:22, play video Jose Mourinho jokes with journalist cousin at presser 2:13 Man wrongly sentenced to death says Met Police ruined his life. 00:02:13, play video Man wrongly sentenced to death says Met Police ruined his life 1:05 How much is rent in Birmingham? 00:01:05, play video How much is rent in Birmingham? 0:51 Inside the control room forecasting your weather. 00:00:51, play video Inside the control room forecasting your weather 1:24 &#x27;It&#x27;s impossible&#x27;: Spain&#x27;s young people on housing. 00:01:24, play video &#x27;It&#x27;s impossible&#x27;: Spain&#x27;s young people on housing 1:32 &#x27;We&#x27;re scared to turn our heating on&#x27; 00:01:32, play video &#x27;We&#x27;re scared to turn our heating on&#x27; 1:02 Why we&#x27;re swapping screens for scenery. 00:01:02, play video Why we&#x27;re swapping screens for scenery 1:09 &#x27;I&#x27;d be dead if it wasn&#x27;t for my binmen&#x27; 00:01:09, play video &#x27;I&#x27;d be dead if it wasn&#x27;t for my binmen&#x27; Top stories Pensions and benefits services still down after &#x27;cyber incident&#x27;\n\nCatherine&#x27;s emotional cancer documentary strips away the stiff upper lip\n\nTwo staff killed in Israeli strike on Gaza medical clinic, MSF says\n\nThe Papers: &#x27;Catherine on cancer fight&#x27; and &#x27;Benefits and pensions chaos&#x27;\n\nA clinic is reassessing people with ADHD and autism diagnoses - but critics fear it could deepen stigma\n\n&#x27;It feels very therapeutic&#x27;: The young hikers swapping screens for walking groups\n\nWeekly quiz: Which music icon&#x27;s house did Miley Cyrus get married at?\n\nMy self-care ritual is a meal at a fancy restaurant - on my own\n\nPregnant US inmate escapes in prison vehicle after medical appointment\n\nThe US pastor who wants to see women&#x27;s right to vote overturned\n\nDesperate appeals from women on death row in Iran after protests\n\nFirst look: Strike and Robin return to take on a dangerous cult\n\nDrift off to sleep with a little help from Bob Mortimer\n\nThe extraordinary journey of the humble banana\n\nHow can vitamin D help us in the winter?\n\nCharity in Capt Tom&#x27;s name bids to be struck off\n\n&#x27;Catherine on cancer fight&#x27; and &#x27;Benefits and pensions chaos&#x27;\n\nAustralian wrestler Bronson Reed dies aged 38, WWE says\n\nPharmacies get new powers to swap prescriptions during shortages\n\nCatherine&#x27;s emotional cancer documentary strips away the stiff upper lip\n\nPensions and benefits services still down after &#x27;cyber incident&#x27;\n\nCarly Rae Jepsen&#x27;s viral hit proves what fans knew all along\n\nSet time aside for children to read for pleasure, schools to be told\n\nA clinic is reassessing people with ADHD and autism diagnoses - but critics fear it could deepen stigma\n\nCopyright © 2026 BBC. The BBC is not responsible for the content of external sites. Read about our approach to external linking.","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cmx2898wq2qjo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss","source_type":"live","vocabPoints":[{"word":"circumstance","phonetic":"","definition":"n. 环境, 状况, 事件","example":"There&#x27;s no discussion or inquiries or anything going on whereby Mr Law would have any ability to appreciate the circumstances of the individual he was dealing with, she said.","example2":"Analysts point to circumstance when explaining the market's reaction.","en_def":"a condition that accompanies or influences some event or activity；information that should be kept in mind when making a decision"},{"word":"prosecution","phonetic":"","definition":"公诉方","example":"If Law receives the prosecution&#x27;s recommended sentence, he would serve just under 20 years in prison, after accounting for credit for time served since his May 2023 arrest.","example2":"The debate over prosecution is unlikely to end soon.","en_def":"the institution and conduct of legal proceedings against a defendant for criminal behavior；the lawyers acting for the state to put the case against the defen…"},{"word":"responsible","phonetic":"","definition":"负责的","example":"Law&#x27;s defence lawyer, Matthew Gourlay, countered those claims, arguing that his client was not responsible for any of their battles with mental illness..","example2":"The report describes the responsible impact on local communities.","en_def":"a. worthy of or requiring responsibility or trust; or held accountable；s. being the agent or cause"}],"grammarPoints":[{"pattern":"现在完成时 (Present Perfect)","explanation":"have/has + 过去分词。表示过去发生、对现在有影响的动作。新闻中常用来强调\"已经发生\"的结果，常与 already、just、so far 连用。","en":"The present perfect (have/has + past participle) links a past action to the present moment. Used when the exact time is not given or the result still matters now: 'Prices have fallen sharply.'","example":"The 61-year-old pleaded guilty in May to 14 counts of aiding the suicide deaths of 14 people in Ontario, Canada, though he has admitted to causing the deaths of dozens of victims in England and Wales.","example2":"Prices have fallen sharply since January, and analysts expect the trend to continue."}],"slangPoints":[{"term":"in a bid to","meaning":"为了；试图（in a bid to end the war）","en":"\"in a bid to\" — a common English phrase; typical collocation: \"in a bid to end the war\".","example":"","example2":"Taxes were cut in a bid to revive the economy."}]},{"id":"n1795854676_4","date":"2026-11-28","source":"BBC","country":"UK","title":"Australian wrestler Bronson Reed dies aged 38, WWE says","summary":"Tributes have flooded in for the 38-year-old Australian who is survived by his pregnant wife, Paige, their daughter.","body":"BBC News Australian wrestler Bronson Reed dies aged 38, WWE says Image source, WWE via Getty Images By Jessica Lamb Published 59 minutes ago Professional wrestler Bronson Reed has died aged 38, World Wrestling Entertainment (WWE) has announced.\n\nThe company said the wrestler, whose real name is Jermaine Haley, died on Friday due to medical complications but did not give further details.\n\nPaying tribute, WWE added: Jermaine was a larger-than-life figure in WWE, but more importantly, he was a phenomenal person, a devoted family man, and a beloved colleague.\n\nThey also offered condolences to the Australian&#x27;s family, including his pregnant wife, Paige, and their daughter.\n\nWWE has been contacted for further comment.\n\nHaley was honoured at the WWE event at Akins Ford Arena in Athens, Georgia on Friday.\n\nThe show began with a 10-bell salute for Haley and in his match, Canadian wrestler Kevin Owens wore an armband that read Bronson and addressed the crowd.\n\nI knew for a fact, beyond performing in this ring, he loved his family more than anything so tonight if there is anything to take away from a tragedy like that - it&#x27;s take care of each other and love each other... Forgive each other, Owens said.\n\nBronson, for me, we were lucky to have you and you will be extremely missed.\n\nWWE greats posted tributes online including the retired 16-time world champion Ric Flair, who wrote: another soldier in our business that left us way too early!\n\nWWE Hall of Famer Diamond Dallas Page said I was deeply saddened to read about the passing of Bronson Reed. I couldn&#x27;t help but think of Eddie. Such a shame. My heart goes out to his friends family. RIP brother.\n\nWWE wrestler Eddie Guerrero - known as Latino Heat - died of heart failure at age 38 in 2005.\n\nImage source, WWE via Getty Images Image caption, Bronson Reed in action against Dragon Lee during RAW in New York in November.\n\nAustralian pro wrestler Brendan Vink, who goes by Duke Hanson, posted I love you, Jermaine. on X.\n\nAccording to wrestling media outlets, Haley had recently been recovering from injuries sustained during the WWE Crown Jewel event in Riyadh, Saudi Arabia earlier in November.\n\nHe did not have a match during the event but was tossed off the stage and through a set of tables during a brawl.\n\nHaley initially competed around the independent wrestling scene as Jonah Rock and J-Rock before signing with WWE in 2019.\n\nHe was released in 2021 before returning a year and continuing with WWE up until his death. In 2024, he won the André the Giant Memorial Battle Royal.\n\nHaley is the second wrestler to die in the last few months after Benjamin Satterley, an English wrestling star passed away at the age of 40 in September.\n\nNo cause of death was provided at the time.\n\nWrestler Benjamin Satterley, known as Pac, dies aged 40\n\nThe video playlist Watch our pick of standout clips from across the BBC\n\nPrevious Next 0:21 The Premier League footballer allergic to grass... 00:00:21, play video The Premier League footballer allergic to grass... 1:16 My 15-year-old son dropped his cup of tea and just died. 00:01:16, play video My 15-year-old son dropped his cup of tea and just died 0:47 Father and son set three driving world records. 00:00:47, play video Father and son set three driving world records 0:55 Co-op Live gig targeted by organised criminals, say police. 00:00:55, play video Co-op Live gig targeted by organised criminals, say police 0:48 What&#x27;s behind the surge in flu? 00:00:48, play video What&#x27;s behind the surge in flu? 1:47 Zelensky questions &#x27;strange&#x27; Trump-Putin friendship. 00:01:47, play video Zelensky questions &#x27;strange&#x27; Trump-Putin friendship 0:58 Suspected rogue trader charged man £9,153 for outdoor socket. 00:00:58, play video Suspected rogue trader charged man £9,153 for outdoor socket 0:35 Four apes die at Twycross Zoo. 00:00:35, play video Four apes die at Twycross Zoo 0:55 Are Black Friday deals really as good as they seem? 00:00:55, play video Are Black Friday deals really as good as they seem? 0:55 Catherine joins cancer support group in new documentary. 00:00:55, play video Catherine joins cancer support group in new documentary 1:20 Student meets man who saved her from Huntingdon train attacker. 00:01:20, play video Student meets man who saved her from Huntingdon train attacker 0:21 Moment semi-truck loses control in US wet weather. 00:00:21, play video Moment semi-truck loses control in US wet weather 0:22 Jose Mourinho jokes with journalist cousin at presser. 00:00:22, play video Jose Mourinho jokes with journalist cousin at presser 2:13 Man wrongly sentenced to death says Met Police ruined his life. 00:02:13, play video Man wrongly sentenced to death says Met Police ruined his life 1:05 How much is rent in Birmingham? 00:01:05, play video How much is rent in Birmingham? 0:51 Inside the control room forecasting your weather. 00:00:51, play video Inside the control room forecasting your weather 1:24 &#x27;It&#x27;s impossible&#x27;: Spain&#x27;s young people on housing. 00:01:24, play video &#x27;It&#x27;s impossible&#x27;: Spain&#x27;s young people on housing 1:32 &#x27;We&#x27;re scared to turn our heating on&#x27; 00:01:32, play video &#x27;We&#x27;re scared to turn our heating on&#x27; 1:02 Why we&#x27;re swapping screens for scenery. 00:01:02, play video Why we&#x27;re swapping screens for scenery 1:09 &#x27;I&#x27;d be dead if it wasn&#x27;t for my binmen&#x27; 00:01:09, play video &#x27;I&#x27;d be dead if it wasn&#x27;t for my binmen&#x27; Top stories Pensions and benefits services still down after &#x27;cyber incident&#x27;\n\nCatherine&#x27;s emotional cancer documentary strips away the stiff upper lip\n\nTwo staff killed in Israeli strike on Gaza medical clinic, MSF says\n\nThe Papers: &#x27;Catherine on cancer fight&#x27; and &#x27;Benefits and pensions chaos&#x27;\n\nA clinic is reassessing people with ADHD and autism diagnoses - but critics fear it could deepen stigma\n\n&#x27;It feels very therapeutic&#x27;: The young hikers swapping screens for walking groups\n\nWeekly quiz: Which music icon&#x27;s house did Miley Cyrus get married at?\n\nMy self-care ritual is a meal at a fancy restaurant - on my own\n\nPregnant US inmate escapes in prison vehicle after medical appointment\n\nThe US pastor who wants to see women&#x27;s right to vote overturned\n\nDesperate appeals from women on death row in Iran after protests\n\nFirst look: Strike and Robin return to take on a dangerous cult\n\nDrift off to sleep with a little help from Bob Mortimer\n\nThe extraordinary journey of the humble banana\n\nHow can vitamin D help us in the winter?\n\nCharity in Capt Tom&#x27;s name bids to be struck off\n\n&#x27;Catherine on cancer fight&#x27; and &#x27;Benefits and pensions chaos&#x27;\n\nAustralian wrestler Bronson Reed dies aged 38, WWE says\n\nPharmacies get new powers to swap prescriptions during shortages\n\nCatherine&#x27;s emotional cancer documentary strips away the stiff upper lip\n\nPensions and benefits services still down after &#x27;cyber incident&#x27;\n\nCarly Rae Jepsen&#x27;s viral hit proves what fans knew all along\n\nSet time aside for children to read for pleasure, schools to be told\n\nA clinic is reassessing people with ADHD and autism diagnoses - but critics fear it could deepen stigma\n\nCopyright © 2026 BBC. The BBC is not responsible for the content of external sites. Read about our approach to external linking.","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cvwy08g70ldko?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss","source_type":"live","vocabPoints":[{"word":"entertainment","phonetic":"","definition":"娱乐","example":"BBC News Australian wrestler Bronson Reed dies aged 38, WWE says Image source, WWE via Getty Images By Jessica Lamb Published 59 minutes ago Professional wrestler Bronson Reed has died aged 38, World Wrestling Entertainm","example2":"The new policy is widely seen as a major step in addressing entertainment.","en_def":"an activity that is diverting and that holds the attention"},{"word":"professional","phonetic":"","definition":"专业的","example":"BBC News Australian wrestler Bronson Reed dies aged 38, WWE says Image source, WWE via Getty Images By Jessica Lamb Published 59 minutes ago Professional wrestler Bronson Reed has died aged 38, World Wrestling Entertainm","example2":"Experts are concerned about the professional change in policy.","en_def":"a person engaged in one of the learned professions；an athlete who plays for pay"},{"word":"complication","phonetic":"","definition":"n. 复杂化, 复杂情况\\ / [医] 并发症, 并发病","example":"The company said the wrestler, whose real name is Jermaine Haley, died on Friday due to medical complications but did not give further details.","example2":"A growing number of people now call attention to complication.","en_def":"the act or process of complicating；a situation or condition that is complex or confused"}],"grammarPoints":[{"pattern":"条件句 (Conditional)","explanation":"if 引导的条件从句。真实条件用一般现在时（if X happens, Y will…）；新闻中也常见虚拟条件表假设。","en":"Conditionals express conditions and results. The first conditional (if + present, will) is used for real future possibilities. Common in policy and economic news.","example":"I knew for a fact, beyond performing in this ring, he loved his family more than anything so tonight if there is anything to take away from a tragedy like that - it&#x27;s take care of each other and love each other...","example2":"If the strike continues into next week, hundreds of flights will be cancelled."}],"slangPoints":[{"term":"draw on","meaning":"利用；借鉴（draw on experience）","en":"\"draw on\" — a common English phrase; typical collocation: \"draw on experience\".","example":"","example2":"The report draws on data from over 40 countries."}]}]};
